{{also|Symptom}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sem-|*peth₂-}}
From {{der|en|grc|σύμπτωμα||a happening, accident, symptom of disease}}, from stem of {{m|grc|συμπίπτω||Ι befall}}, from {{m|grc|συν-||together}} + {{m|grc|πίπτω||I fall}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsɪm(p)təm/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-symptom.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{hyph|en|symp|tom}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|medicine}} A [[perceived]] [[change]] in some [[function]], [[sensation]] or [[appearance]] of a [[person]] that [[indicates]] a [[disease]] or [[disorder]], such as [[fever]], [[headache]] or [[rash]]; strictly, a symptom is felt or experienced by the patient, while a [[sign]] can be detected by an observer.
#: {{ux|en|Swollen breasts, morning sickness, and a missed period are classic '''symptoms''' of pregnancy.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A [[signal]]; anything that [[indicate]]s, or is [[characteristic]] of, the [[presence]] of something else, especially of something undesirable.
#* {{RQ:Landon Ethel Churchill|pages=114–115|volume=I|passage="Nay, nay," exclaimed Lord Norbourne, "I cannot go quite so far as that. I have, thanks to your hospitality, laid in a stock of health enough for the ensuing winter: but as to the general benevolence of which you talk, I confess I find no '''symptoms''': if I did, they would alarm me more than those of the gout."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Charles Zastrow|title=Introduction to Social Work and Social Welfare|passage=Some people see birth outside of marriage as a social problem—a sign of a breakdown in the traditional family and a '''symptom''' of moral decay.}}
#: {{ux|en|Lying, hiding one's true feelings, and having affairs are typical '''symptoms''' of a doomed marriage.}}

